---
// The Self-healing tab: automation that came out of on-call issues. Two of them can be played with
// here, each under the short version of its story, and two more are told without a simulation.
import DriftSim from '../../components/DriftSim.astro';
import Onward from '../../components/Onward.astro';
import StorageSim from '../../components/StorageSim.astro';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Self-healing: issues that fix themselves before an outage"
  path="/self-healing"
  description="Automation I built at Hughes that runs on a schedule, decides whether it is safe to act, makes the repair and records it. Two are simulated here with made-up systems."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Automation I built at Hughes · two you can play with</p>
      <h1>Issues that now fix themselves before they become outages. Break a node and watch.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        These run on a schedule, decide whether it is safe to act, make the repair and record it. Two are simulated
        here with made-up systems.
      </p>
      <nav class="on-this-page" aria-label="On this page">
        <span class="eyebrow">On this page</span>
        <a href="#storage">Storage monitor, with a simulation</a>
        <a href="#drift">Config drift, with a simulation</a>
        <a href="#more">Two more</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <section class="section" id="storage" aria-labelledby="storage-title">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">01 · Storage self-healing monitor</p>
        <h2 id="storage-title">A 90-minute manual recovery, now zero touch</h2>
      </div>
      <article class="case surface">
        <h3>A storage failure, about every two weeks</h3>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div class="bad">
            <dt>The outage</dt>
            <dd>
              About 40,000 to 60,000 users offline on the newest satellite system, depending on which SNC rack was
              affected.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="warn">
            <dt>By hand</dt>
            <dd>About an hour and a half of manual recovery each time.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What I built</dt>
            <dd>A monitor that moves the work to a standby when the standby has room, and refuses when it does not.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="good">
            <dt>Now</dt>
            <dd>
              <strong>Caught on a 3-minute check and fixed before customers lose service, with no human involved.</strong>
            </dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <a class="go" href="/self-healing/storage">How it decides, in detail <span aria-hidden="true">&nbsp;→</span></a>
      </article>
      <div class="play">
        <StorageSim />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="drift" aria-labelledby="drift-title">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">02 · Config drift self-heal</p>
        <h2 id="drift-title">Two racks that must agree, checked every ten minutes</h2>
      </div>
      <article class="case surface">
        <h3>A modem failover while two racks disagreed, about once a month</h3>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div class="bad">
            <dt>The outage</dt>
            <dd>
              The SNC rack had new configuration and the RF gateway rack, which holds the modems, still had the old
              one. A modem failed over onto that mismatch and about 20,000 to 30,000 residential HughesNet customers
              lost their internet service.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="warn">
            <dt>By hand</dt>
            <dd>Two hours to find and fix by hand each time.</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What I built</dt>
            <dd>
              A checker that compares the two racks’ configuration every ten minutes and restarts the affected service
              when they disagree.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="good">
            <dt>Now</dt>
            <dd><strong>89% of episodes close inside one cycle.</strong></dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <a class="go" href="/self-healing/config-drift">How it works, in detail <span aria-hidden="true">&nbsp;→</span></a>
      </article>
      <div class="play">
        <DriftSim />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="more" aria-labelledby="more-title">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Two more, without a simulation</p>
        <h2 id="more-title">The same habit, twice more</h2>
      </div>
      <article class="case surface">
        <h3>Heavy weather forces a failover to the spare RF gateway</h3>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div class="bad">
            <dt>The outage</dt>
            <dd>
              An RF gateway is the large antenna that talks to the satellite. In heavy weather its traffic fails over
              to a diverse (spare) gateway, whose modems must be rebooted to carry it. If they are not, 20,000 to
              30,000 customers lose service.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="warn">
            <dt>By hand</dt>
            <dd>
              Around an hour for engineers to work out what had happened, get to the affected modems and reboot them
              one by one, by logging in and running ‘reboot’ or pressing the button on each modem’s web page.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What I built</dt>
            <dd>
              A check that runs every minute. One API tells it a gateway failover has happened. A second reboots the
              modems on the spare gateway’s rack. Every action is written to PostgreSQL.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="good">
            <dt>Now</dt>
            <dd><strong>A failover is noticed within a minute and the modems are rebooted automatically.</strong></dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="made-with mono soft">asyncio · httpx · SQLAlchemy + psycopg2 · PostgreSQL · loguru</p>
      </article>
      <article class="case surface">
        <h3>Logs filling the storage, then log messages going missing</h3>
        <dl class="facts">
          <div class="bad">
            <dt>The problem</dt>
            <dd>Storage use was high because of the sheer volume of logs.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="warn">
            <dt>What I found</dt>
            <dd>
              Every automation wrote its logs into its own script folder instead of one shared place. The first fix,
              rotation and retention configured inside loguru, brought a second problem: log messages were seen going
              missing.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>What I did</dt>
            <dd>
              Made loguru the standard, with every automation logging to
              <code>/var/log/Automations/&lt;name&gt;.log</code>. I took rotation and retention out of the loguru setup
              and gave both to a logrotate rule with <code>copytruncate</code>, which loguru cannot do itself and which
              rotates a file without losing messages.
            </dd>
          </div>
          <div class="good">
            <dt>Now</dt>
            <dd><strong>Log footprint down from 19.68 GB to 58 MB.</strong></dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </article>
      <p class="stack mono soft" id="stack">
        How each automation is built: Python run with uv. asyncio with asyncssh to read from devices and run healing
        commands over SSH, httpx for APIs, SQLAlchemy and psycopg2 for PostgreSQL, loguru for logs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Onward
      links={[
        { label: 'Where it started', text: 'One 2 AM incident, step by step', href: '/on-call' },
        { label: 'Before this', text: 'The scripts an engineer ran by hand', href: '/tools' },
        { label: 'Outside work', text: 'Three side projects you can open and check', href: '/projects' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The heading here is two sentences, not a title: it gets a longer line than the shared 20ch. */
  .page-head h1 {
    max-width: 32ch;
  }

  /* Inside the page head an eyebrow takes the accent, which is for the page's own label, not this one. */
  .on-this-page .eyebrow {
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  /* The simulation is its own piece under the story, so it stands further off than a card does. */
  .play {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }

  .case + .case,
  .stack {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  /* What a card's automation is made of, as its last line. */
  .made-with {
    padding: var(--space-3) 1.25rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .stack {
    max-width: 51rem;
  }
</style>
